--00000000000065a4d3065dfa4413
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="UTF-8"

I wanted to suggest a clarification of Section 10, titled "Application Data
Protocol". As of draft 02, the text of this section is the same as in RFC
9147. The first sentence says that "Application data messages are carried
by the record layer and are split into records ...". This seems to indicate
that DTLS will fragment large application protocol messages into multiple
records and UDP datagrams. However, that can't be right, because
non-handshake messages don't have the fragment offsets and lengths that
would be necessary to reassemble messages that had been split up.
Presumably application protocols would not be prepared to handle one
message on the sending side turning into two smaller messages on the
receiving side.

I think this section should be changed to remove mention of splitting up
messages, and instead say that each message is encapsulated in a record.
It's possible the application protocol itself may have its own mechanism
for fragmenting and reassembling large messages, but if the document
mentions that, it should be made clear that this is not part of DTLS itself.

I hope this helps, please let me know if I misunderstood anything.
Thanks,
--David Cook

